  "_meta": {
    "title": "Japanese era names",
    "scope": "241 entries covering 645 CE to the present, from Taika to Reiwa, of which two mark gaps when no era name was in use. Reckoning runs continuously under one imperial line.",
    "line": "A single continuous line with no dynastic divisions. For the period of the two courts, 1331 to 1392, the northern line is followed and the era names of the southern court are not collected.",
    "yearSpace": "Astronomical year numbering. Every entry falls in the common era, so no negative years occur.",
    "fields": "y = the calendar year this entry takes effect. n = era name, written in present-day Japanese forms. gap = true means no era name was in use from this year until the year given by the next entry.",
    "provenance": "Compiled and checked by hand against the standard Japanese chronological tables. Historical chronology is not itself copyrightable; the choice of the northern line, the explicit treatment of gaps, the field design and the transcription are original to this project.",
    "count": {"eras": 241},
    "license": "CC-BY-SA-4.0",
    "updated": "2026-07-17",
    "caveats": [
      "Taika, in 645, is the first Japanese era name. Nothing before that year can be named this way.",
      "Two genuine gaps fall between 645 and 701, from the lapse of Hakuchi in 654 until Shucho in 686, and from the lapse of Shucho in 687 until Taiho in 701. They are marked as gap entries rather than filled by carrying the preceding name forward.",
      "An era rarely changed at the turn of the year, so one calendar year often spans two era names. The table gives the one in principal use that year, which leaves a known error of one year at the boundary: 749 is entered as Tenpyo-shoho and 1185 as Bunji.",
      "For 1331 to 1392 only the northern line appears. Southern court eras such as Engen, Kokoku and Shohei are absent.",
      "Reiwa, from 2019, is the current era and has no closing year, so every later year is counted within it. This is a simplification, not a prediction that no further era will be declared.",
      "Entries are in ascending order of y, and each applies until the year given by the next entry."
    ]
  },
